speechtext:
Mr. Speaker, I am pleased to respond to the question posed by my colleague, the hon. member for Parkdale-High Park (Mr. Flis). He knows quite a bit about the subject and there is no point in my reviewing what he already knows.
As he has stated, the Export Finance Corporation of Canada, Ltd. was incorporated by special act of Parliament in 1959. It was founded by the major Canadian banks. Through a
December 3, 1981

series of mergers, the present shareholders are the Bank of Montreal, the National Bank of Canada, the Bank of Nova Scotia, the Toronto-Dominion Bank, the Canadian Imperial Bank of Commerce, the Mercantile Bank of Canada and the Royal Bank of Canada.
The underlying intention of the special act was to involve the chartered banks in medium-term export financing. In 1968 the operations of the Export Finance Corporation of Canada, Ltd. became inactive owing to prevailing circumstances at that time.
Section 6 of the act includes the following wording:
The corporation, in the furtherance of its objects and not excluding the other powers necessary to their achievement, may
(A)	Purchase, discount, rediscount, sell, negotiate with or without its endorsement or guarantee notes, drafts, cheques;
(B)	Purchase and sell securities, coin, bullion and exchange and guarantee securities;
(C)	Make or underwrite a loan or a series of loans;
(D)	Participate with buyer and seller in an export-import transaction;
(E)	Deal with financing applications for resale to a lender within or outside Canada on a commission or fee basis;
(F)	Purchase with its funds preferred or common stock in any other corporation.
The corporation was active until 1969 when circumstances were such that the corporation ceased active operations, but has maintained its corporate existence by making yearly filings of its annual summaries and financial statements with the Department of Consumer and Corporate Affairs.
Each year since that date the directors have discussed the advisability of reactivating the corporation. They intend to keep it in existence until such time as it can be usefully reactivated. While the corporation was active, it operated successfully. As an example, the 1963 annual report of the chairman of the Export Credits Insurance Corporation, now replaced by the Export Development Corporation, indicated that during that year, the Export Credits Insurance Corporation sold to Export Finance Corporation of Canada, Ltd. $27 million of notes it held which were guaranteed by the Export Insurance Corporation. In 1964 similar sales of notes were made to Export Finance Corporation of Canada, Ltd. in the amount of $23 million.
In April 1969 the president of Export Finance Corporation of Canada, Ltd. spoke with officials of the Export Credits Insurance Corporation concerning the pending creation of the Export Development Corporation. It would appear that interest rates and the replacement of the Export Credits Insurance Corporation by the Export Development Corporation were among the factors causing Export Finance Corporation of Canada, Ltd. to become inactive. The corporation remains in good standing as a special act corporation.
